WEBVTT

00:00:12.428 --> 00:00:14.472
An important room, this.

00:00:14.514 --> 00:00:16.474
After all,
it is a chocolate factory.

00:00:16.516 --> 00:00:18.393
Then why is the door
so small?

00:00:18.476 --> 00:00:21.979
That’s to keep all the great
big chocolaty flavor inside.

00:00:22.021 --> 00:00:23.523
( giggles )

00:00:31.155 --> 00:00:32.949
( doors creaking )

00:00:44.001 --> 00:00:48.256
Now... do be careful,
my dear children.

00:00:48.339 --> 00:00:49.507
Don’t lose your heads.

00:00:49.590 --> 00:00:52.176
Don’t get overexcited.

00:00:52.218 --> 00:00:55.721
Just keep very calm.

00:01:00.268 --> 00:01:03.521
( gurgling )

00:01:11.696 --> 00:01:13.322
It’s beautiful.

00:01:13.364 --> 00:01:15.616
What?

00:01:15.700 --> 00:01:18.703
Ooh, yeah,
it’s very beautiful.

00:01:27.420 --> 00:01:30.465
Every drop of the river

00:01:30.548 --> 00:01:34.302
is hot melted chocolate
of the finest quality.

00:01:34.385 --> 00:01:38.431
( waterfall rumbling )

00:01:38.514 --> 00:01:41.601
The waterfall is most important.

00:01:41.684 --> 00:01:43.561
It mixes the chocolate,

00:01:43.644 --> 00:01:46.981
churns it up,
makes it light and frothy.

00:01:47.064 --> 00:01:48.232
By the way,

00:01:48.316 --> 00:01:52.278
no other factory in the world
mixes its chocolate

00:01:52.361 --> 00:01:54.280
by waterfall, my dear children.

00:01:54.363 --> 00:01:56.741
And you can take that
to the bank.

00:02:06.918 --> 00:02:09.170
People!

00:02:10.213 --> 00:02:12.465
Those pipes...

00:02:12.548 --> 00:02:14.509
suck up the chocolate

00:02:14.592 --> 00:02:17.637
and carry it away
all over the factory.

00:02:17.720 --> 00:02:20.181
Thousands of gallons
an hour.

00:02:20.264 --> 00:02:21.224
Yeah.

00:02:21.265 --> 00:02:23.267
And do you like
my meadow?

00:02:23.351 --> 00:02:25.436
Try some of my grass.

00:02:25.478 --> 00:02:26.771
Please have a blade. Please do.

00:02:26.854 --> 00:02:30.191
It’s so delectable
and so darn good-looking.

00:02:30.274 --> 00:02:32.276
You can eat
the grass?

00:02:32.360 --> 00:02:34.195
Of course you can.

00:02:34.278 --> 00:02:35.196
Everything

00:02:35.279 --> 00:02:37.031
in this room
is eatable.

00:02:37.114 --> 00:02:38.199
Even I’m eatable,

00:02:38.282 --> 00:02:41.118
but that is called cannibalism,
my dear children,

00:02:41.160 --> 00:02:44.497
and is, in fact, frowned upon
in most societies.

00:02:44.580 --> 00:02:46.165
Yeah.

00:02:46.249 --> 00:02:48.042
Enjoy.

00:02:48.125 --> 00:02:49.961
Go on.

00:02:50.002 --> 00:02:51.712
Scoot, scoot.

00:03:04.225 --> 00:03:05.643
Hmm.

00:03:05.685 --> 00:03:07.687
( grunting )

00:03:11.691 --> 00:03:12.483
( shouts )

00:03:12.525 --> 00:03:13.651
Son...

00:03:13.693 --> 00:03:15.069
( growls )

00:03:15.152 --> 00:03:16.821
Please...

00:03:16.862 --> 00:03:21.200
Dad, he said enjoy.

00:03:21.284 --> 00:03:22.326
( grunts )

00:03:34.297 --> 00:03:37.550
Why hold onto it?
Why not start a new piece?

00:03:37.633 --> 00:03:39.677
Because then I wouldn’t be
a champion.

00:03:39.760 --> 00:03:43.014
I’d be a loser like you.

00:03:48.185 --> 00:03:49.979
( spits )

00:03:58.738 --> 00:04:00.990
( candy burbles )

00:04:11.000 --> 00:04:13.669
Daddy, look over there.

00:04:14.462 --> 00:04:16.839
What is it?

00:04:16.881 --> 00:04:18.591
It’s a little person.

00:04:18.674 --> 00:04:21.385
Over there,
by the waterfall.

00:04:21.427 --> 00:04:23.304
( jackhammer pattering )

00:04:23.387 --> 00:04:24.388
There’s two of them.

00:04:24.472 --> 00:04:26.265
There’s more
than two.

00:04:31.812 --> 00:04:34.065
MRS. GLOOP:
Where do they come from?

00:04:34.148 --> 00:04:35.191
Who are they?

00:04:36.734 --> 00:04:37.860
Are they real people?

00:04:37.902 --> 00:04:39.904
Of course
they’re real people.

00:04:39.945 --> 00:04:41.322
They’re Oompa-Loompas.

00:04:44.700 --> 00:04:45.701
Oompa-Loompas?

00:04:45.743 --> 00:04:47.745
Imported direct
from Loompaland.

00:04:47.787 --> 00:04:49.246
There’s no
such place.

00:04:49.330 --> 00:04:50.247
What?

00:04:50.331 --> 00:04:53.376
Mr. Wonka, I teach
high school geography,

00:04:53.417 --> 00:04:54.585
and I’m here
to tell you...

00:04:54.669 --> 00:04:56.087
Well, then you’ll know
all about it,

00:04:56.128 --> 00:05:00.216
and know what
a terrible country it is.

00:05:10.101 --> 00:05:12.478
WONKA:
The whole place is nothing
but thick jungles

00:05:12.561 --> 00:05:16.941
infested by the most dangerous
beasts in the entire world:

00:05:16.982 --> 00:05:19.443
Hornswagglers and snozwangers

00:05:19.485 --> 00:05:21.987
and those terrible,
wicked wangdoodles.

00:05:22.071 --> 00:05:25.324
( insect buzzing )

00:05:35.751 --> 00:05:38.003
( insect squeals )

00:05:42.383 --> 00:05:44.009
I went to Loompaland

00:05:44.093 --> 00:05:48.305
looking for exotic
new flavors for candy.

00:05:50.182 --> 00:05:54.729
Instead, I found
the Oompa-Loompas.

00:06:02.236 --> 00:06:04.321
They lived in tree houses
to escape

00:06:04.363 --> 00:06:07.992
from the fierce creatures
who lived below.

00:06:10.661 --> 00:06:13.831
The Oompa-Loompas ate nothing
but green caterpillars

00:06:13.914 --> 00:06:15.875
which tasted revolting.

00:06:16.917 --> 00:06:18.085
The Oompa-Loompas
kept looking for

00:06:18.169 --> 00:06:19.545
other things to mash up

00:06:19.628 --> 00:06:22.006
with the caterpillars
to make them taste better:

00:06:22.047 --> 00:06:25.468
red beetles, the bark
of the bong-bong tree--

00:06:25.509 --> 00:06:27.136
all of them beastly,

00:06:27.178 --> 00:06:30.431
but not quite so beastly
as the caterpillars.

00:06:35.519 --> 00:06:36.562
( sniffs )

00:06:36.645 --> 00:06:37.897
Mm.

00:06:37.980 --> 00:06:38.898
( stifled gag )

00:06:38.981 --> 00:06:40.441
( laughs )

00:06:45.446 --> 00:06:47.156
( thick sloshing )

